# OSC strings (T49). EXPECT = replies only, and the answer is almost always
# silence — which is the point.
#
# ST is written \ rather than \: the case reader takes a backslash
# literally, so a doubled one is two bytes and leaves a stray backslash in the
# stream.
#
# OSC 8 and OSC 52 do not reply to the terminal's output stream; they hand an
# action to the app layer. What these cases pin is that nothing here ever
# answers BACK to the remote side unless it has been permitted to, and that a
# malformed or oversized string leaves the parser working.

# A well-formed hyperlink is consumed, and says nothing to the server
IN \x1B]8;id=42;https://example.test/a\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]8;;\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -

# A clipboard WRITE is consumed silently
IN \x1B]52;c;aGVsbG8=\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -

# A clipboard READ is not answered. Permission is per session and off by
# default (rules/net.md), and the refusal is SILENT: replying "no" still tells
# a remote program that a clipboard is there to ask about.
IN \x1B]52;c;?\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -

# OSC 9;4 taskbar progress IS implemented (T67) and, like OSC 133, answers
# NOTHING. It is a report to the window manager, not a query — a terminal that
# replied would be telling a remote program whether it has a taskbar.
#
# READ WHAT THESE PIN, AND WHAT THEY DO NOT. This category's sink implements
# oscStart/oscPut/oscEnd as no-ops (harness.cpp), so nothing here ever builds an
# OscHandler: these cases would pass unchanged if parseProgress were deleted.
# What they prove is the pair of things this file exists for — that the bytes
# are consumed silently, and that the parser is left usable afterwards (the DA1
# below is the proof of the second). Nothing more, and for a while nothing else
# in the corpus pinned more: the GRAMMAR is pinned by corpus/shell/progress.case
# (whose sink runs a real Session and reads the OscAction back through onOsc)
# and by `[core][osc]` in tests/unit/osc_test.cpp. Verified by mutation rather
# than assumed — neutering parseProgress fails 23 assertions there and none
# here.
IN \x1B]9;4;1;50\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]9;4;0\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]9;4;3\x07
EXPECT -

# ...and the malformed forms are silent for the ordinary reason: nothing to say.
# What they must NOT do is leave the parser confused, which the DA1 pins.
IN \x1B]9;4;9;50\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]9;5;1;50\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]9;this is a notification\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B[c
EXPECT reply:\x1B[?1;2c

# Codes we have not implemented stay silent rather than half-handled
IN \x1B]4;1;rgb:00/00/00\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]10;?\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]7;file:///tmp\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -

# OSC 133 IS implemented (T66, see corpus/shell/) and still answers NOTHING.
# A prompt mark is an assertion, not a query, and a terminal that replied to
# one would be handing a remote program a probe for whether shell integration
# is on. What it does instead is entirely on the grid line.
IN \x1B]133;A\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]133;D;1\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -

# Aborted mid-string (CAN). xterm discards these and so do we — and, more to
# the point, the parser comes back cleanly afterwards.
IN \x1B]52;c;aGVsbG8=\x18
EXPECT -
IN \x1B[c
EXPECT reply:\x1B[?1;2c

# Interrupted by a new introducer rather than a terminator
IN \x1B]8;;https://example.test\x1B[c
EXPECT reply:\x1B[?1;2c

# BEL terminates an OSC as well as ST
IN \x1B]0;title\x07
EXPECT -
IN \x1B[c
EXPECT reply:\x1B[?1;2c

# Empty and degenerate strings are not actions
IN \x1B]\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B];;;\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]52\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B]52;\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -

# Invalid base64 is refused whole, never decoded as far as it goes
IN \x1B]52;c;not base64!\x1B\x5C
EXPECT -
IN \x1B[c
EXPECT reply:\x1B[?1;2c
